export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"plant","name":"Plant","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"plant-start","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: getting started","summary":"What the Plant add-on does in BimCraft, how to switch it on, and what it leaves alone.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["plant","process","factory","pipe spec","isometric","equipment","nozzle","layout","piping","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-newer-bimcraft","text":"What needs a newer BimCraft","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlant is an add-on for BimCraft that comes with it and is free. It adds four things for process and factory work: \u003Cstrong\u003Epipe specs\u003C/strong\u003E checked against the pipe runs in the model, \u003Cstrong\u003Eisometric drawings\u003C/strong\u003E of pipe runs, \u003Cstrong\u003Eprocess equipment\u003C/strong\u003E with nozzles saved as an IFC file, and a \u003Cstrong\u003Elayout check\u003C/strong\u003E for aisles, the space beside each item and floor load.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab of the right panel, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe spec\u003C/span\u003E section when you select a pipe run) and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drafting aid, not an engineering approval.\u003C/strong\u003E The specs, the equipment shapes and the limits are generic and ours: the sizes and walls come from the public tables, the equipment is drawn in the right proportions but with no maker&#039;s dimensions, the aisle and floor-load limits are yours to set. The person responsible for the plant decides what is right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-newer-bimcraft\"\u003EWhat needs a newer BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPutting equipment into the open model (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E button in the equipment table) and the router&#039;s list of ports need BimCraft&#039;s add-on API 1.1, which this build has; in a build without it the Plant tab says so, and the equipment goes out as its own IFC file, which BimCraft opens like any other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet: the router finds a route between two ports and shows its corners, but putting the run into the model waits for a run writer in the same API; spool numbers wait for BimCraft&#039;s own (the isometric draws balloons when the runs carry them); the layout check reads the equipment table, not equipment placed from the library.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on changes the model only when you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E (one undo step); everything else reads it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Plant is an add-on for BimCraft that comes with it and is free. It adds four things for process and factory work: pipe specs checked against the pipe runs in the model, isometric drawings of pipe runs, process equipment with nozzles saved as an IFC file, and a layout check for aisles, the space beside each item and floor load. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Plant. Its tools are in the Plant tab of the right panel, in Properties (a Pipe spec section when you select a pipe run) and in Export. A drafting aid, not an engineering approval. The specs, the equipment shapes and the limits are generic and ours: the sizes and walls come from the public tables, the equipment is drawn in the right proportions but with no maker's dimensions, the aisle and floor-load limits are yours to set. The person responsible for the plant decides what is right. What needs a newer BimCraft Putting equipment into the open model (the Place button in the equipment table) and the router's list of ports need BimCraft's add-on API 1.1, which this build has; in a build without it the Plant tab says so, and the equipment goes out as its own IFC file, which BimCraft opens like any other. Not built yet: the router finds a route between two ports and shows its corners, but putting the run into the model waits for a run writer in the same API; spool numbers wait for BimCraft's own (the isometric draws balloons when the runs carry them); the layout check reads the equipment table, not equipment placed from the library. The add-on changes the model only when you press Place (one undo step); everything else reads it."},{"slug":"plant-specs","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: pipe specs","summary":"What a pipe spec is, the five that come with the add-on, and how a pipe run is checked against its spec.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["pipe spec","specification","class","size","valve","branch","bend","elbow","steel","copper","pvc","pex"],"headings":[{"id":"what-a-spec-is","text":"What a spec is","level":2},{"id":"check-a-run","text":"Check a run","level":2},{"id":"branches-and-bends","text":"Branches and bends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-spec-is\"\u003EWhat a spec is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pipe spec is a class of pipe the way a plant writes it: the material and wall, the smallest and largest size, the valves it may carry and at which sizes, whether its elbows are long or short radius, and how a branch is made off a header of each size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFive come with the add-on, written from the public size tables (steel to ASME B36.10M, copper to ASTM B88, PVC to ASTM D1785, PEX to ASTM F876): carbon steel Sch 40 (\u003Ccode\u003ECS40\u003C/code\u003E) and Sch 80 (\u003Ccode\u003ECS80\u003C/code\u003E), copper type L (\u003Ccode\u003ECU-L\u003C/code\u003E), PVC Sch 40 (\u003Ccode\u003EPVC40\u003C/code\u003E) and PEX (\u003Ccode\u003EPEX9\u003C/code\u003E). They are general-service starting points and ours; a plant&#039;s own spec book overrides them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run is matched to its spec by the \u003Cstrong\u003Ematerial and class\u003C/strong\u003E it already carries, so nothing has to be added to a run to check it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-a-run\"\u003ECheck a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a pipe run: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe spec\u003C/span\u003E section — the spec, the sizes it allows, whether this run&#039;s size and each valve are allowed, the bends, and how branches of 4:1, 2:1 and 1:1 are made. Select several runs and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the selected pipe runs\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab to list every finding.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinding\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELevel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo pipe spec\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe run&#039;s material and class match no spec\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESize not allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe run&#039;s size is outside the spec&#039;s sizes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EValve not allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe spec does not allow that kind of valve at that size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"branches-and-bends\"\u003EBranches and bends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA branch up to a quarter of its header&#039;s size is a stub-in on the steel specs; larger ones are reducing tees; equal sizes are tees. A branch larger than its header is not a branch. A long-radius elbow&#039;s centre line is 1.5 × the nominal size; a short one is one nominal size (at least the outside diameter).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What a spec is A pipe spec is a class of pipe the way a plant writes it: the material and wall, the smallest and largest size, the valves it may carry and at which sizes, whether its elbows are long or short radius, and how a branch is made off a header of each size. Five come with the add-on, written from the public size tables (steel to ASME B36.10M, copper to ASTM B88, PVC to ASTM D1785, PEX to ASTM F876): carbon steel Sch 40 (CS40) and Sch 80 (CS80), copper type L (CU-L), PVC Sch 40 (PVC40) and PEX (PEX9). They are general-service starting points and ours; a plant's own spec book overrides them. A run is matched to its spec by the material and class it already carries, so nothing has to be added to a run to check it. Check a run Select a pipe run: Properties shows a Pipe spec section — the spec, the sizes it allows, whether this run's size and each valve are allowed, the bends, and how branches of 4:1, 2:1 and 1:1 are made. Select several runs and press Check the selected pipe runs in the Plant tab to list every finding. Finding Level Means No pipe spec warning the run's material and class match no spec Size not allowed error the run's size is outside the spec's sizes Valve not allowed error the spec does not allow that kind of valve at that size Branches and bends A branch up to a quarter of its header's size is a stub-in on the steel specs; larger ones are reducing tees; equal sizes are tees. A branch larger than its header is not a branch. A long-radius elbow's centre line is 1.5 × the nominal size; a short one is one nominal size (at least the outside diameter)."},{"slug":"plant-iso","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: isometric drawings","summary":"Save the isometric of selected pipe runs as SVG or DXF, with dimensions, elevations, valves, welds, spool balloons and a bill of materials.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["isometric","iso","drawing","dimension","elevation","bill of materials","bom","spool","weld","svg","dxf"],"headings":[{"id":"make-one","text":"Make one","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-it","text":"What is on it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"make-one\"\u003EMake one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one or more pipe runs in the model, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EIsometric of the selected pipe runs\u003C/strong\u003E (SVG or DXF), or the buttons in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E saves the list on its own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-it\"\u003EWhat is on it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe pipe on its centre line in the usual 30° projection: east to the right and up, north to the left and up, up stays up. The north arrow shows the plant&#039;s north.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dimension on every leg: its true length along the pipe, in millimetres, or feet and inches for a drawing in inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe elevation at each end and where the run changes level; the run&#039;s tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA weld dot at each elbow, valves as symbols turned along the pipe, a flow arrow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpool balloons where the runs carry spool numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA bill of materials: pipe length by size and spec, elbows by angle, valves by kind, welds. A title block with the project, drawing number, revision, drawn by and date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot to scale.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing says so. It is fitted to an A3 sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe DXF opens in CadCraft and other CAD programs: lines, circles, solid fills and text on layers named \u003Ccode\u003EISO-PIPE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-DIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-SYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-TEXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-BOM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EISO-FRAME\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Make one Select one or more pipe runs in the model, then Export › Isometric of the selected pipe runs (SVG or DXF), or the buttons in the Plant tab. Bill of materials (CSV) saves the list on its own. What is on it The pipe on its centre line in the usual 30° projection: east to the right and up, north to the left and up, up stays up. The north arrow shows the plant's north. A dimension on every leg: its true length along the pipe, in millimetres, or feet and inches for a drawing in inches. The elevation at each end and where the run changes level; the run's tag. A weld dot at each elbow, valves as symbols turned along the pipe, a flow arrow. Spool balloons where the runs carry spool numbers. A bill of materials: pipe length by size and spec, elbows by angle, valves by kind, welds. A title block with the project, drawing number, revision, drawn by and date. Not to scale. The drawing says so. It is fitted to an A3 sheet. The DXF opens in CadCraft and other CAD programs: lines, circles, solid fills and text on layers named ISO-PIPE, ISO-DIM, ISO-SYMBOL, ISO-TEXT, ISO-BOM and ISO-FRAME."},{"slug":"plant-equipment","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: process equipment and layout","summary":"Add generic equipment with nozzles, save it as an IFC file with a port at every nozzle, and check the layout for aisles, access and floor load.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["equipment","vessel","tank","pump","exchanger","column","nozzle","port","ifc","layout","aisle","clearance","floor load"],"headings":[{"id":"equipment-with-nozzles","text":"Equipment with nozzles","level":2},{"id":"in-the-open-model","text":"In the open model","level":2},{"id":"route-a-pipe","text":"Route a pipe","level":2},{"id":"layout-check","text":"Layout check","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"equipment-with-nozzles\"\u003EEquipment with nozzles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab keeps a table of equipment in this browser: vertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, column, centrifugal pump, shell and tube exchanger and plate exchanger. Choose a type, give it a tag, a place (X, Y, Z in millimetres), a turn about Z and, if you know it, a weight, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach is drawn in closed solids of the right proportions and has \u003Cstrong\u003Enozzles\u003C/strong\u003E: where a pipe meets it, with a position, the direction the pipe leaves in, a size and a flow direction. They are generic shapes — no maker&#039;s dimensions — so use your vendor&#039;s figures where they matter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC file\u003C/span\u003E writes the table as an IFC4 file: each item as the right class (a pump, a tank, a heat exchanger), its solids, its tag, and one distribution port per nozzle with its flow direction and size, so the pipe runs of any program can connect to it. Open the file in BimCraft like any other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-open-model\"\u003EIn the open model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on a row (or the &quot;Place equipment in the model&quot; command, for every row not placed yet) writes the item into the open model through BimCraft&#039;s add-on API: the equipment, its tag and the property set \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Plant\u003C/code\u003E, and a port at every nozzle with its size in \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Nozzle\u003C/code\u003E. It lands on the model&#039;s first storey at the place in the table; it is one undo step, and Undo takes the equipment and its ports out again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"route-a-pipe\"\u003ERoute a pipe\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoute a pipe\u003C/span\u003E lists the ports of the model. Choose two, a spec and a size and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind a route\u003C/span\u003E: the router leaves each nozzle straight out, runs only along X, Y and Z, keeps clear of the equipment you placed, never bends twice within two pipe diameters (150 mm at least) and prefers few bends and few climbs. The result is its length, its bends and its corners, in the storey&#039;s own millimetres. Writing the run into the model is not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layout-check\"\u003ELayout check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the layout\u003C/span\u003E looks at the table for: items that \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverlap\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003Eaisles\u003C/strong\u003E narrower than your limit (900 mm to start with); the \u003Cstrong\u003Espace beside each item\u003C/strong\u003E that must stay free (800 mm in front, 300 mm elsewhere to start with) when something stands in it; and \u003Cstrong\u003Efloor load\u003C/strong\u003E over your limit (1000 kg/m² to start with). The limits are numbers you set; they are not a code requirement, and the check is a drafting aid.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Equipment with nozzles The Plant tab keeps a table of equipment in this browser: vertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, column, centrifugal pump, shell and tube exchanger and plate exchanger. Choose a type, give it a tag, a place (X, Y, Z in millimetres), a turn about Z and, if you know it, a weight, and press Add. Each is drawn in closed solids of the right proportions and has nozzles: where a pipe meets it, with a position, the direction the pipe leaves in, a size and a flow direction. They are generic shapes — no maker's dimensions — so use your vendor's figures where they matter. Save as IFC file writes the table as an IFC4 file: each item as the right class (a pump, a tank, a heat exchanger), its solids, its tag, and one distribution port per nozzle with its flow direction and size, so the pipe runs of any program can connect to it. Open the file in BimCraft like any other. In the open model Place on a row (or the \"Place equipment in the model\" command, for every row not placed yet) writes the item into the open model through BimCraft's add-on API: the equipment, its tag and the property set CadCraft_Plant, and a port at every nozzle with its size in CadCraft_Nozzle. It lands on the model's first storey at the place in the table; it is one undo step, and Undo takes the equipment and its ports out again. Route a pipe Route a pipe lists the ports of the model. Choose two, a spec and a size and press Find a route: the router leaves each nozzle straight out, runs only along X, Y and Z, keeps clear of the equipment you placed, never bends twice within two pipe diameters (150 mm at least) and prefers few bends and few climbs. The result is its length, its bends and its corners, in the storey's own millimetres. Writing the run into the model is not built yet. Layout check Check the layout looks at the table for: items that overlap; aisles narrower than your limit (900 mm to start with); the space beside each item that must stay free (800 mm in front, 300 mm elsewhere to start with) when something stands in it; and floor load over your limit (1000 kg/m² to start with). The limits are numbers you set; they are not a code requirement, and the check is a drafting aid."}]};
